  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 231 AM EDT Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A foggy start is expected across the region this morning \nas saturated soils, light winds, and clear skies help promote for \nformation. With sunrise shortly after 5:30 AM, it's likely most fog \nwill dissipate prior to the morning commute. Still, some patchy \ndense fog in sheltered valleys and river valleys could continue \nthrough 9 AM. Afterwards, we've got some beautiful weather on tap as \nsurface high pressure noses in from the west. Mostly clear skies \nwith highs in the upper 70s to mid 80s are expected both today and \nSaturday with an isolated shower or two across the higher peaks of \nthe Adirondacks. Limited mid-level steering flow will keep the \nshowers anchored across the higher terrain and will be short-lived \nas increasing subsidence will kill any convection that tries to \ndevelop. Northwest winds less than 10 mph will be seen today with \nwinds backing to the south at 5 to 15 mph on Saturday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Quite a bit has changed in the last 24 hours as \ndeterministic guidance is struggling to understand the evolution of \na deepening upper level trough/low and short-wave energy. The latest \ntrends show a strengthening upper level trough that ultimately closes \noff as shortwave energy rounds the base of the upper level trough \nand increases vorticity. This ultimately leads to a higher amplitude \nlongwave trough which slows down the progression of both the deep \nmoisture and better frontal dynamics. The general thinking is that \nmost of Sunday is dry; albeit a bit humid as dewpoints start \napproaching 70 degrees. Rainfall is now expected to move in Sunday \nnight from west to east and continue well into Monday as a surface \nlow develops across western New York and quickly move north towards \nMontreal. A plume of 1.7\" to 1.9\" PWATs is still expected to advect \nnorthward across New England during this timeframe which should \nsupport efficient rain processes. The general thinking of 0.5 to 1.5 \ninches of rainfall still seems likely and the flood threat seems \nmore contingent on rainfall rates vs actual rainfall amounts. \nLooking at the GFS and ECMWF ensembles, there are still about 10-20 \npercent of members showing over 2 inches of rainfall so there are \nstill some concerns to watch out for as we head into early next \nweek. The picture should become more clear tomorrow as we get better \nupper air sampling which should help the models begin locking in on \na solution vs being so volatile from run-to-run.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: A building low-mid level ridge in the wake of the \nnorthern stream shortwave trough departing the area to the east will \nyield warming temperatures Tuesday (8/4) through Thursday (8/6) next \nweek. GEFS 850mb mean temperatures support valley high temperatures \nin the lower 80s for Tuesday, and then in the upper 80s for \nWednesday and Thursday. With increasing humidity through the period, \nthe best chance of thunderstorms will likely be Thursday, with PoPs \n40-50% for Thursday afternoon. Otherwise, no significant weather is \ncurrently anticipated during the Days 4-7 forecast period.",
